		</Folder>
		<Folder>
			<name>Midway &amp; Lower Geyser Basins</name>
			<description>Many more geysers, hot springs, and colorful pools are in these areas, most with boardwalk interpretive trails. 

Rudyard Kipling immortalized Midway Basin in this basin in 1889 by calling it &quot;Hell&apos;s Half Acre&quot;. Lower Geyser Basin has a large assortment of mud pots, geysers, pools, springs, and fumaroles.</description>
			<View>
				<longitude>-110.8243208887083</longitude>
				<latitude>44.54577033063958</latitude>
				<range>7774.006382896907</range>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<heading>-24.84353336957472</heading>
			</View>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Fountain Paint Pot</name>
				<description>Mud pots are found at the highest points in a hydrothermal basin. That puts them above the water table, so they contain only water from condensed steam or the ground surface. The water becomes acidic, breaking down rocks and minerals to form mud. Over many years, a muddy spring becomes a mud pot.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8059097294513</longitude>
					<latitude>44.54982122305975</latitude>
					<range>2496.707166152921</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.83062629485719</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8061536898104,44.55010874746802,2269.062602127885</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Pocket Basin Mud Pots</name>
				<description>The largest collection of mud pots in Yellowstone, Pocket Basin Mud Pots is an isolated group with no designated access to this very fragile and delicate area. The basin was formed by a hydrothermal explosion, a result from overheated ground water that exploded into steam, leaving a crater-like basin.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8334777202668</longitude>
					<latitude>44.56204902273374</latitude>
					<range>3115.907935636422</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.84996963542147</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8334779322101,44.56205125470066,2208.518144703599</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Excelsior Geyser Crater</name>
				<description>Excelsior is the third largest geyser in the world, and was once considered the park&apos;s most powerful geyser. It&apos;s now dormant since its last known major eruptions during the 1880s, although it did erupt again in 1985 for two days. Today&apos;s it&apos;s considered a hot spring, a productive thermal spring, discharging more than 4050 gallons of boiling water per minute.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8365580875532</longitude>
					<latitude>44.52655441050229</latitude>
					<range>2544.338275781578</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85211430573223</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8366679170963,44.52629402133499,2255.088861332332</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Grand Prismatic Spring</name>
				<description>The largest hot spring in Yellowstone and considered to be third largest in the world, named for its rainbow-like colors. It has a deep blue center followed by pale blue, green algae forms along the shallow edge, and the outside has a scalloped rim of yellow fading to orange, with a red outer border. Steam often shrouds the spring which reflects the brilliant colors. Grand Prismatic discharges an estimated 560 gallons of water per minute.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.838091900918</longitude>
					<latitude>44.52474260030038</latitude>
					<range>2624.921307552748</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85318981759962</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
