				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Porcelain Basin</name>
				<description>Porcelain Basin has hundreds of densely packed geothermal features in contrast to more forested areas. Rainbow colors, hissing steam, and pungent odors combine to create this unique area in Yellowstone. A number of these geysers and other features have been born suddenly due to hydrothermal explosions. Some features last only a few hours, days or weeks. Some are permanent features, such as Blue Geyser.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.703599720842</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72826924442511</latitude>
					<range>2821.231708968644</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>0.001425803812239061</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7033877693377,44.72882721164086,2341.343483244858</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Porkchop Geyser</name>
				<description>Once a small hot spring that occasionally erupted, Porkchop Geyser became a continuous spouter in the spring of 1985. The force of the spray caused a boisterous roar that could be heard from the museum. On September 5, 1989 Porkchop exploded and propelled rocks more than 200 feet from the feature. It&apos;s now a gently boiling hot spring, and a prime example of the unpredictable features of Norris Geyser Basin.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.7070334020189</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72220300551481</latitude>
					<range>2608.809394051246</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-22.5205637167422</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7076006313143,44.7223344759553,2338.638124790279</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Echinus Geyser</name>
				<description>Until 1997, Echinus erupted every 35-75 minutes and most visitors were able to witness this geyser in full action. Its pools filled gradually with water; then suddenly burst as steam and water exploded 40-60 feet upward. Echinus is the largest acid-water geyser known. Acid geysers are extremely rare, however most of the planet&apos;s acid geysers are found in the Norris Geyser Basin. Since 1999 Echinus&apos; eruptions have been weak and erratic, but it may return to full glory at any time.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.7020228536417</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72196416526334</latitude>
					<range>2494.512594234441</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>13.63607645425888</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7021577361586,44.72202862078377,2349.973745382872</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Emerald Spring</name>
				<description>Clear blue water combines with the yellow sulfur coated crater to create a breathtaking green color. This spring is 27 feet deep and the average temperature is 190 degrees F.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.7042225387407</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72574378549218</latitude>
					<range>2540.157464079884</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-82.41223160480715</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7044118735011,44.72550576240707,2339.266340958789</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Steamboat Geyser</name>
				<description>The world&apos;s tallest active geyser, Steamboat can erupt to more than 300 feet, showering viewers with its mineral-rich waters. For hours following its rare 3 to 40 minute major eruptions, Steamboat thunders with powerful jets of steam. As befitting such an awesome event, full eruptions are entirely unpredictable. In recent years, Steamboat has had three major eruptions. More commonly, Steamboat has minor eruptions and ejects water in frequent bursts of 10 to 40 feet.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.7030362420715</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72348250850941</latitude>
					<range>2635.118857712711</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.75825935972932</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7033367279051,44.72353166505377,2341.669770875982</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Norris Museum</name>
				<description>Built in 1929-30 and a National Historic Landmark, the museum&apos;s distinctive stone-and-log architecture became a prototype for park buildings throughout the country known as &quot;parkitecture&quot;. Exhibits include geothermal geology, Norris Geyser Basin features, and life in thermal areas.  The Museum of the National Park Ranger is nearby.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.7035423693427</longitude>
					<latitude>44.72636990320128</latitude>
					<range>2477.09820383304</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-4.976156008500371</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon44.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.7037395797047,44.72649015228932,2340.153632543565</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
		</Folder>
		<Folder>
			<name>Upper Geyser Basin, Old Faithful</name>
			<description>A few of the more famous and scenic geysers and hot spring pools are included in this folder. There are excellent boardwalk interpretive trails through the geysers and pools in this area, as well as a large visitor center, the historic Old Faithful Inn, and other amenities.</description>
			<View>
				<longitude>-110.8373956063619</longitude>
				<latitude>44.46584623464283</latitude>
				<range>5305.992048945947</range>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<heading>-24.85270499466289</heading>
			</View>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Black Sand Basin</name>
				<description>Named for obsidian fragments in the sand, this area has a small collection of jewel-like geysers and colorful hot springs. Emerald Pool is the most colorful and famous of these springs, with a deep emerald green color with yellow and orange fringes forming the outer ring of the pool. The clear water of the pool reflects the blues but asborbs the other hues of the color spectrum, and the combination produces its green color. The outer ring is a fringe of yellow and orange, where the temperatures are cooler (154.6 degrees F.) due to a change in bacteria and algae growth. 

Other colorful geothermal pools here include Sunset Lake, Rainbow Pool, Opalescent, and Handkerchief - so named because people used to drop handkerchiefs in one vent and they&apos;d resurface in another, hot-water rinsed. Spouter and Cliff Geysers are also in this area.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8492333918092</longitude>
					<latitude>44.46488437215487</latitude>
					<range>3720.43007111551</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.86099774246139</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8492331004259,44.46488432769191,2263.493859576509</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Beauty and Chromatic Pools</name>
				<description>Beauty and Chromatic pools are connected underground. Over a period of weeks one pool gets hotter and begins to overflow as the other gets cooler and stops overflowing. Then the process reverses. Usually the pool that is overflowing is the most colorful.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8392708226843</longitude>
					<latitude>44.4693178184816</latitude>
					<range>2690.456904032149</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85401905454848</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8392520164793,44.46909968495692,2275.793680580694</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Daisy Geyser</name>
				<description>Daisy eruptions are unique in that they erupt on an angle, and reach a height of 75 feet. Daisy is predictable with eruptions occurring every 90-115 minutes.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8429031659088</longitude>
					<latitude>44.47031021163469</latitude>
					<range>2613.728943422063</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85656378520882</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8430804056787,44.47030996400937,2268.25467513486</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Morning Glory Pool</name>
				<description>Morning Glory was one the most glorious pool in the park, hence the name. Unfortunately most of its beauty has been destroyed through visitors throwing rocks, coins, trash, logs, and other debris which has clogged the natural plumbing of the pool. Water is unable to circulate and therefore thermal energy is lost. The drop in temperature has allowed yellow bacteria to grow in Morning glory. it has been mostly destroyed the beautiful, clear, colorful spring. The ultimate example of the impact humans have on nature.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8432849141391</longitude>
					<latitude>44.47509289900801</latitude>
					<range>2499.76396157902</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85683121648408</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8436829393589,44.4750275073791,2274.990242356108</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Castle Geyser</name>
				<description>Castle may be the oldest geyser in the Upper Geyser Basin. It also has the largest cone of any geyser. Eruptions reach 90 ft and last approximately 20 minutes. The water phase is followed by steam, which can last 30-40 minutes. Castle Geyser erupts every 10-12 hours.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8363744763242</longitude>
					<latitude>44.46369450863167</latitude>
					<range>2623.401851419966</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85199043017182</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8365176944822,44.46358874068257,2285.037481286341</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Lion Group Geysers</name>
				<description>Four separate geysers that are all interconnected underground: the Lion, Lioness, Big Cub and Little Cub. The Lion exhibits the largest eruptions. This geyser family got its name from the deep roaring sound the geyser makes after an eruption has occurred.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8295416183955</longitude>
					<latitude>44.46356169697823</latitude>
					<range>3227.544906634837</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.84720403479504</heading>
				</View>
				<styleUrl>#default+nicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon63.png+hicon=http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal3/icon55.png</styleUrl>
				<Style>
					<IconStyle>
						<heading>0</heading>
						<Icon>
							<href>http://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/pal4/icon47.png</href>
						</Icon>
					</IconStyle>
				</Style>
				<Point>
					<coordinates>-110.8303965870503,44.46348072363205,2295.573886727772</coordinates>
				</Point>
			</Placemark>
			<Placemark>
				<name>Grand Geyser</name>
				<description>Often overshadowed by Old Faithful, Grand Geyser is the largest, most predictable geyser in the world. Grand erupts from a large pool with powerful gusts. Eruptions occur every 7-15 hours and last 9-12 minutes.</description>
				<View>
					<longitude>-110.8364880167381</longitude>
					<latitude>44.46652287855374</latitude>
					<range>2958.984079307631</range>
					<tilt>0</tilt>
					<heading>-24.85206991636534</heading>
